{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AeLrreKB7Fa8tuCe22chis","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd011-magefiles\n     2→title: \"magefiles and cmd/version: Build Lifecycle and Version Identity\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  go-unix-utils has no testable artifact until its first utility passes differential tests.\n     6→  The mage build targets (build, lint, clean, install) are inherited from cobbler-scaffold's\n     7→  orchestrator library, but the project has no specification for what those targets must do\n     8→  in this repository. Without a PRD the build lifecycle is an undocumented dependency of\n     9→  every other PRD.\n    10→\n    11→  A second gap is version identity. The project tags documentation releases (v0.YYYYMMDD.N)\n    12→  and code releases (v1.YYYYMMDD.N), but no binary reports which version it was built from.\n    13→  A cmd/version binary that prints the last known version tag gives a concrete, testable\n    14→  artifact that exercises the full build pipeline (compile, lint, test, install) before any\n    15→  utility code exists. It also provides a version string that other cmd/ binaries can import\n    16→  when they need to report their own version.\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Specify a cmd/version binary that prints the repository's last known version tag.\n    20→  - G2: Specify the build target that compiles all cmd/ binaries to bin/.\n    21→  - G3: Specify the lint target that runs golangci-lint on all Go source.\n    22→  - G4: Specify the clean target that removes build artifacts.\n    23→  - G5: Specify the install target that runs go install for cmd/ binaries.\n    24→  - G6: Specify the configuration contract between magefiles/ and configuration.yaml for the fields consumed by these targets.\n    25→\n    26→requirements:\n    27→  R1:\n    28→    title: cmd/version binary\n    29→    items:\n    30→      - R1.1: |\n    31→          Must provide a cmd/version/ package that compiles to a standalone binary. When\n    32→          invoked with no arguments, the binary prints the version string to stdout followed\n    33→          by a newline and exits 0.\n    34→      - R1.2: |\n    35→          The version string must be set at build time via -ldflags \"-X main.version=<tag>\".\n    36→          When the linker variable is not set (development builds), the binary must print\n    37→          \"dev\" as the version string.\n    38→      - R1.3: |\n    39→          The mage build target must resolve the version tag by running\n    40→          git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match \"v[0-9]*\" and passing the result to the\n    41→          linker. If the git command fails (no tags, not a git repository), the build must\n    42→          succeed with the version set to \"dev\".\n    43→      - R1.4: |\n    44→          When invoked with --version or -v, the binary must print the same version string\n    45→          as the no-argument invocation. Any other flag must cause the binary to print a\n    46→          usage message to stderr and exit 2.\n    47→      - R1.5: |\n    48→          The version package must export a variable or function so that other cmd/ packages\n    49→          can import and report the same version string without duplicating the ldflags\n    50→          mechanism.\n    51→\n    52→  R2:\n    53→    title: Build target\n    54→    items:\n    55→      - R2.1: |\n    56→          mage build must compile every package under cmd/ to a binary in the directory\n    57→          specified by configuration.yaml project.binary_dir (default: bin/). Each binary\n    58→          is named after its cmd/ subdirectory (e.g., cmd/version/ produces bin/version).\n    59→      - R2.2: |\n    60→          mage build must pass -ldflags with the version tag resolved per R1.3 to every\n    61→          cmd/ binary that imports the version variable.\n    62→      - R2.3: |\n    63→          mage build must fail with a non-zero exit code if any cmd/ package fails to\n    64→          compile. The error message must identify which package failed.\n    65→      - R2.4: |\n    66→          mage build must create the binary_dir if it does not exist.\n    67→\n    68→  R3:\n    69→    title: Lint target\n    70→    items:\n    71→      - R3.1: |\n    72→          mage lint must run golangci-lint on all Go source directories listed in\n    73→          configuration.yaml project.go_source_dirs (default: pkg, cmd). The linter\n    74→          must also cover magefiles/.\n    75→      - R3.2: |\n    76→          mage lint must fail with a non-zero exit code if golangci-lint reports any\n    77→          warnings or errors.\n    78→      - R3.3: |\n    79→          The golangci-lint configuration must be committed to the repository (as\n    80→          .golangci.yml or equivalent) so that lint results are reproducible.\n    81→\n    82→  R4:\n    83→    title: Clean target\n    84→    items:\n    85→      - R4.1: |\n    86→          mage clean must remove the binary_dir and all its contents. If the directory\n    87→          does not exist, the target must succeed silently.\n    88→      - R4.2: |\n    89→          mage clean must not remove source files, documentation, or any directory\n    90→          outside binary_dir.\n    91→\n    92→  R5:\n    93→    title: Install target\n    94→    items:\n    95→      - R5.1: |\n    96→          mage install must run go install for every package under cmd/, installing\n    97→          binaries to the Go bin directory ($GOPATH/bin or $GOBIN).\n    98→      - R5.2: |\n    99→          mage install must pass the same -ldflags as mage build (R2.2).\n   100→\n   101→  R6:\n   102→    title: Configuration contract\n   103→    items:\n   104→      - R6.1: |\n   105→          The following fields in configuration.yaml project section are consumed by the\n   106→          build lifecycle targets and must be present:\n   107→\n   108→            project.module_path    — Go module path (used by go build)\n   109→            project.binary_dir     — output directory for compiled binaries (default: bin)\n   110→            project.go_source_dirs — list of directories containing Go source (default: [pkg, cmd])\n   111→            project.magefiles_dir  — directory containing mage targets (default: magefiles)\n   112→      - R6.2: |\n   113→          magefiles/orchestrator.go must load configuration.yaml at init time via the\n   114→          orchestrator library. If the file does not exist, the init function must create\n   115→          a default configuration file and log the action to stderr.\n   116→      - R6.3: |\n   117→          Build lifecycle targets (build, lint, clean, install) must not depend on fields\n   118→          outside the project section of configuration.yaml. Orchestrator, cobbler, and\n   119→          claude sections are consumed by cobbler-scaffold targets, not by this PRD's scope.\n   120→\n   121→non_goals:\n   122→  - This PRD does not specify orchestrator targets (cobbler:measure, cobbler:stitch). Those are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n   123→  - This PRD does not specify generator lifecycle targets (generator:start, generator:run, generator:stop). Those are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n   124→  - This PRD does not specify scaffold, prompt, or stats targets. Those are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n   125→  - This PRD does not specify the analyze target. Cross-artifact consistency checks are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n   126→  - cmd/version does not parse LS_COLORS, handle signals, or do anything beyond printing a version string.\n   127→\n   128→acceptance_criteria:\n   129→  - \"AC1: mage build compiles cmd/version/ to bin/version. Running bin/version prints a version tag string (matching v[0-9]*) followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\n   130→  - \"AC2: bin/version --version prints the same output as bin/version with no arguments.\"\n   131→  - \"AC3: bin/version --bogus prints a usage message to stderr and exits 2.\"\n   132→  - \"AC4: A development build (without ldflags) produces a binary that prints \\\"dev\\\".\"\n   133→  - \"AC5: mage lint runs golangci-lint on cmd/, pkg/, and magefiles/ with no warnings.\"\n   134→  - \"AC6: mage clean removes bin/ and succeeds silently when bin/ does not exist.\"\n   135→  - \"AC7: mage build followed by mage clean followed by ls bin/ shows bin/ does not exist.\"\n   136→  - \"AC8: All configuration fields consumed by build lifecycle targets are documented in R6.1 and present in configuration.yaml.\"\n   137→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51c1bdc4-3982-4ec7-82b7-bc8d63e68421","uuid":"73e47bbb-e7c2-4a59-8957-22d73790f905","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/105/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd011-magefiles.yaml","content":"id: prd011-magefiles\ntitle: \"magefiles and cmd/version: Build Lifecycle and Version Identity\"\n\nproblem: |\n  go-unix-utils has no testable artifact until its first utility passes differential tests.\n  The mage build targets (build, lint, clean, install) are inherited from cobbler-scaffold's\n  orchestrator library, but the project has no specification for what those targets must do\n  in this repository. Without a PRD the build lifecycle is an undocumented dependency of\n  every other PRD.\n\n  A second gap is version identity. The project tags documentation releases (v0.YYYYMMDD.N)\n  and code releases (v1.YYYYMMDD.N), but no binary reports which version it was built from.\n  A cmd/version binary that prints the last known version tag gives a concrete, testable\n  artifact that exercises the full build pipeline (compile, lint, test, install) before any\n  utility code exists. It also provides a version string that other cmd/ binaries can import\n  when they need to report their own version.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Specify a cmd/version binary that prints the repository's last known version tag.\n  - G2: Specify the build target that compiles all cmd/ binaries to bin/.\n  - G3: Specify the lint target that runs golangci-lint on all Go source.\n  - G4: Specify the clean target that removes build artifacts.\n  - G5: Specify the install target that runs go install for cmd/ binaries.\n  - G6: Specify the configuration contract between magefiles/ and configuration.yaml for the fields consumed by these targets.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: cmd/version binary\n    items:\n      - R1.1: |\n          Must provide a cmd/version/ package that compiles to a standalone binary. When\n          invoked with no arguments, the binary prints the version string to stdout followed\n          by a newline and exits 0.\n      - R1.2: |\n          The version string must be set at build time via -ldflags \"-X main.version=<tag>\".\n          When the linker variable is not set (development builds), the binary must print\n          \"dev\" as the version string.\n      - R1.3: |\n          The mage build target must resolve the version tag by running\n          git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match \"v[0-9]*\" and passing the result to the\n          linker. If the git command fails (no tags, not a git repository), the build must\n          succeed with the version set to \"dev\".\n      - R1.4: |\n          When invoked with --version or -v, the binary must print the same version string\n          as the no-argument invocation. Any other flag must cause the binary to print a\n          usage message to stderr and exit 2.\n      - R1.5: |\n          The version package must export a variable or function so that other cmd/ packages\n          can import and report the same version string without duplicating the ldflags\n          mechanism.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Build target\n    items:\n      - R2.1: |\n          mage build must compile every package under cmd/ to a binary in the directory\n          specified by configuration.yaml project.binary_dir (default: bin/). Each binary\n          is named after its cmd/ subdirectory (e.g., cmd/version/ produces bin/version).\n      - R2.2: |\n          mage build must pass -ldflags with the version tag resolved per R1.3 to every\n          cmd/ binary that imports the version variable.\n      - R2.3: |\n          mage build must fail with a non-zero exit code if any cmd/ package fails to\n          compile. The error message must identify which package failed.\n      - R2.4: |\n          mage build must create the binary_dir if it does not exist.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Lint target\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          mage lint must run golangci-lint on all Go source directories listed in\n          configuration.yaml project.go_source_dirs (default: pkg, cmd). The linter\n          must also cover magefiles/.\n      - R3.2: |\n          mage lint must fail with a non-zero exit code if golangci-lint reports any\n          warnings or errors.\n      - R3.3: |\n          The golangci-lint configuration must be committed to the repository (as\n          .golangci.yml or equivalent) so that lint results are reproducible.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Clean target\n    items:\n      - R4.1: |\n          mage clean must remove the binary_dir and all its contents. If the directory\n          does not exist, the target must succeed silently.\n      - R4.2: |\n          mage clean must not remove source files, documentation, or any directory\n          outside binary_dir.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Install target\n    items:\n      - R5.1: |\n          mage install must run go install for every package under cmd/, installing\n          binaries to the Go bin directory ($GOPATH/bin or $GOBIN).\n      - R5.2: |\n          mage install must pass the same -ldflags as mage build (R2.2).\n\n  R6:\n    title: Configuration contract\n    items:\n      - R6.1: |\n          The following fields in configuration.yaml project section are consumed by the\n          build lifecycle targets and must be present:\n\n            project.module_path    — Go module path (used by go build)\n            project.binary_dir     — output directory for compiled binaries (default: bin)\n            project.go_source_dirs — list of directories containing Go source (default: [pkg, cmd])\n            project.magefiles_dir  — directory containing mage targets (default: magefiles)\n      - R6.2: |\n          magefiles/orchestrator.go must load configuration.yaml at init time via the\n          orchestrator library. If the file does not exist, the init function must create\n          a default configuration file and log the action to stderr.\n      - R6.3: |\n          Build lifecycle targets (build, lint, clean, install) must not depend on fields\n          outside the project section of configuration.yaml. Orchestrator, cobbler, and\n          claude sections are consumed by cobbler-scaffold targets, not by this PRD's scope.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - This PRD does not specify orchestrator targets (cobbler:measure, cobbler:stitch). Those are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n  - This PRD does not specify generator lifecycle targets (generator:start, generator:run, generator:stop). Those are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n  - This PRD does not specify scaffold, prompt, or stats targets. Those are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n  - This PRD does not specify the analyze target. Cross-artifact consistency checks are provided by cobbler-scaffold.\n  - cmd/version does not parse LS_COLORS, handle signals, or do anything beyond printing a version string.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: mage build compiles cmd/version/ to bin/version. Running bin/version prints a version tag string (matching v[0-9]*) followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\n  - \"AC2: bin/version --version prints the same output as bin/version with no arguments.\"\n  - \"AC3: bin/version --bogus prints a usage message to stderr and exits 2.\"\n  - \"AC4: A development build (without ldflags) produces a binary that prints \\\"dev\\\".\"\n  - \"AC5: mage lint runs golangci-lint on cmd/, pkg/, and magefiles/ with no warnings.\"\n  - \"AC6: mage clean removes bin/ and succeeds silently when bin/ does not exist.\"\n  - \"AC7: mage build followed by mage clean followed by ls bin/ shows bin/ does not exist.\"\n  - \"AC8: All configuration fields consumed by build lifecycle targets are documented in R6.1 and present in configuration.yaml.\"\n","numLines":137,"startLine":1,"totalLines":137}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015oQCudwG1G9Y9Fod5s9vUB","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n     2→// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n     3→\n     4→// Tests for terminal size query functions.\n     5→//\n     6→// Implements: prd002-sys R1.1, R1.2\n     7→package sys\n     8→\n     9→import (\n    10→\t\"os\"\n    11→\t\"testing\"\n    12→)\n    13→\n    14→// --- TerminalSize non-TTY error path (prd002-sys R1.1, R1.2) ---\n    15→\n    16→func TestTerminalSize_NonTTY(t *testing.T) {\n    17→\tr, w, err := os.Pipe()\n    18→\tif err != nil {\n    19→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"os.Pipe: %v\", err)\n    20→\t}\n    21→\tdefer r.Close()\n    22→\tdefer w.Close()\n    23→\n    24→\torigStdout := os.Stdout\n    25→\tos.Stdout = w\n    26→\tdefer func() { os.Stdout = origStdout }()\n    27→\n    28→\tcols, rows, err := TerminalSize()\n    29→\tif err == nil {\n    30→\t\tt.Errorf(\"TerminalSize() on pipe: expected error, got cols=%d rows=%d\", cols, rows)\n    31→\t}\n    32→}\n    33→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51c1bdc4-3982-4ec7-82b7-bc8d63e68421","uuid":"f46b3a5e-6740-4796-9f0f-a63643e5dd98","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/105/pkg/sys/terminal_test.go","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n// Tests for terminal size query functions.\n//\n// Implements: prd002-sys R1.1, R1.2\npackage sys\n\nimport (\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\n// --- TerminalSize non-TTY error path (prd002-sys R1.1, R1.2) ---\n\nfunc TestTerminalSize_NonTTY(t *testing.T) {\n\tr, w, err := os.Pipe()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"os.Pipe: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tdefer r.Close()\n\tdefer w.Close()\n\n\torigStdout := os.Stdout\n\tos.Stdout = w\n\tdefer func() { os.Stdout = origStdout }()\n\n\tcols, rows, err := TerminalSize()\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"TerminalSize() on pipe: expected error, got cols=%d rows=%d\", cols, rows)\n\t}\n}\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YYZx6EQhS7kiGwSGsiUUuQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the context I need. Let me create the test file.\n\nKey points:\n1. Package `main` (same as the implementation) so we can access unexported `run()` function\n2. Capture stdout/stderr using `os.Pipe()` pattern from terminal_test.go\n3. Test 4 scenarios:\n   - No args: prints version, returns 0\n   - Default value is \"dev\" (since no ldflags)\n   - `--version` and `-v` flags: print version, return 0\n   - Unrecognized arg: prints usage to stderr, returns 2\n\nThe `run()` function uses `fmt.Println(version)` which writes to `os.Stdout`, and `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...)` for errors. So I need to capture both.\n\n\nSince the version variable defaults to \"dev\" without ldflags, I'll use table-driven tests to verify the four scenarios: no arguments, `--version` flag, `-v` flag, and an unrecognized argument. I'll redirect stdout and stderr using pipes to capture and validate the output and exit codes.\n\nWriting test helper functions... Writing test cases... I'm reviewing the actual requirements from the PRD more carefully—R1.5 is about exporting the version for other packages to use, not about handling unrecognized arguments, so there's a mismatch between what the task description says and what the PRD actually specifies. I need to clarify which requirements I'm actually testing against before writing the test cases. I see that R1.4 actually covers both valid flags and unrecognized arguments, so the task is splitting this into separate test cases even though they're technically from the same requirement. I'll just implement what's being asked regardless of the PRD numbering. Now I'm setting up the test file with helpers to capture both stdout and stderr simultaneously so I can verify the correct output is going to the right stream.\n\nWriting test implementation... Writing helper functions...\n\nNow I'm setting up test cases for the Run function, defining the expected behavior when no arguments are provided—it should print the version string and exit with code 0, with \"dev\" as the default version when the linker variable isn't set.\n\nContinuing with more test cases... I'm running into a nesting problem with the capture functions, so I need a better approach—I'll create a helper that captures both stdout and stderr simultaneously since `run()` only writes to one at a time.\n\nWriting the capture helper... I'm distinguishing between the two requirements by having the R1.1 test use a custom version string to verify the command prints whatever version is set, while the R1.2 test explicitly checks that the default version is \"dev\" when no linker variable is provided. Now I'm writing out the complete test file with proper copyright headers and test implementations.\n\nWriting test cases... Writing test cases... Finishing the test assertions and verifying the test suite covers all the acceptance criteria—the test file compiles, runs successfully, and validates all four scenarios with proper exit codes and output matching.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3072,"cache_read_input_tokens":36391,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3072},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51c1bdc4-3982-4ec7-82b7-bc8d63e68421","uuid":"ee208506-b819-465d-b09c-1443d2432f93"}
